 "takeaways": [
  [
   "Retraining taught the hard decisions",
   "On the questions where the raw odds mislead, the Game Theorist went from 11% to 82% and the Gut Player from 24% to 87%. Four of the five learners now score higher on the whole paper than their first version did. The Exploiter scores lower."
  ],
  [
   "It did not make them harder to exploit",
   "At a four-player table with 2 dice each, the hunter wins more against today's Gut Player (45% before, 57% now), Exploiter (80%, 83%) and Game Theorist (57%, 69%) than against their first versions. Only the Old-Schooler and the Hybrid got harder to exploit."
  ],
  [
   "A high mark is not the same as safe",
   "The first Exploiter scored 99% on the answer key, yet the hunter beats it in 95% of duels. It plays good moves but always the same ones, so a counter-strategy can be built around them. That is why the exam needs both papers."
  ]
 ],
 "method": "Paper A covers one-on-one rounds with 1 die each and with 2 dice against 1. The answer key is within 0.002 points of perfect play, measured by what a best reply could still gain. A move counts as correct if it gives up at most half a point of round-win chance. Paper B is an exact best reply to each bot's move chances, playing whole games down from the starting dice. The hunter knows the bot's habits exactly, so its score is a worst case, not what a person at the table would get. As a spot check, several cases from each paper were also played out for 20,000 to 30,000 real games with the code that runs the table, and matched the computed figures."
}